# LonghuaCRM Project Rules

## Tech Stack

- Backend: NestJS
- ORM: TypeORM
- Database: PostgreSQL
- Language: TypeScript
- Package manager: npm

## Architecture

Always preserve existing business logic.

Never rewrite working functionality unless explicitly requested.

Prefer incremental refactoring over large rewrites.

Never introduce breaking changes without explaining them.

Always follow NestJS best practices.

Business logic belongs in Services.

Controllers should remain thin.

Use dependency injection.

Never duplicate business logic.

## Database

Never introduce json_record or JSON/JSONB storage for stable business entities.

Always use relational entities.

Every database schema change must include a TypeORM migration.

Never enable synchronize=true.

When modifying an Entity, always verify:

- Entity relations
- Repository
- Service
- Controller
- DTOs
- Validation decorators
- Imports
- Migrations

## Code Quality

Always use strict typing.

Avoid any unless absolutely unavoidable.

Reuse existing services whenever possible.

Remove dead code after refactoring.

Do not leave TODO or FIXME comments unless explicitly requested.

## Validation

Validate every incoming DTO.

Use class-validator decorators.

Never trust client input.

## Error Handling

Use NestJS exceptions.

Provide meaningful error messages.

Avoid silent failures.

## Security

Never generate SQL vulnerable to injection.

Never expose secrets.

Always validate external input.

Escape user-controlled values where appropriate.

## Refactoring

When changing one module, check for affected modules.

Update imports automatically.

Fix all compilation errors.

Do not stop after the first error.

## Build

After every significant change:

1. Run npm run build
2. Fix every compilation error
3. Repeat until the project builds successfully

Do not finish the task while the project does not compile.

## Response Style

When making architectural changes:

- explain why
- explain trade-offs
- keep changes minimal
- preserve backward compatibility whenever possible